"The surah begins by mentioning the îlâf of Quraysh. The spelling of the word 'îlâf' varies according to different recitations (qira'at); in some it is written with a hamzah, and in others with the letter 'ya'. These differences point to the different pronunciations and meanings of the word. The original spelling in the mushafs of 'Uthman is without an 'alif' and with a 'ya', and this form encompasses all three recitations simultaneously.\n\nÎlâf derives from the root 'ulfah'. In the if'al form, it means 'to accustom, to make inclined', and in the mufa'alah form, it means 'to agree, to enter into a covenant'. It expresses both Quraysh's mutual agreements among themselves and their commercial and political agreements with those around them. It is not correct to reduce this word to a single meaning; it encompasses the meanings of both familiarity (ulfah) and covenant-making.\n\nQuraysh is the great Arab tribe to which the Prophet (peace be upon him) also belonged. Its lineage traces back to Fihr b. Malik b. al-Nadr b. Kinanah. Its name is derived from the word 'taqarrush', which means 'gathering, coming together' or 'engaging in trade'. This diminutive expression is actually intended to denote greatness and honor. Thanks to leaders such as Qusayy and Hashim, Quraysh distinguished itself from the other tribes in Mecca and attained a preeminent position.\n\nThe letter 'lam' at the beginning of the surah has been interpreted in three ways. First, it is for ta'ajjub (wonder/astonishment): expressing surprise that Quraysh fails to give thanks despite these blessings. Second, it is for ta'lil (expressing cause/reason): it is linked either to the action in the preceding surah (the destruction of the companions of the elephant) or to the command 'let them worship'. The preferred view is that the 'lam' serves as ta'lil for the command 'let them worship'. Third, this lam recalls the past and future îlâf of Quraysh, especially the treaties that ensured the security of the pilgrimage and trade routes.\n\nThe winter and summer journeys of Quraysh were travels undertaken for the purpose of trade. In winter they would go to Yemen, and in summer to al-Sham (Syria). Hashim and his brothers made agreements with neighboring kingdoms for the safety of these journeys, securing immunity for the merchants of Quraysh. These agreements were virtually in the nature of capitulations. The Event of the Elephant reinforced this order and enhanced the prestige of Quraysh.\n\nAllah bestowed favor upon Quraysh by both saving them from hunger (nourishment through trade) and granting them security from a great fear (the attack of the elephant army). In return for these blessings, Quraysh must worship the Lord of this House (the Kaaba), abandon polytheism (shirk), and adhere to the religion of tawhid (monotheism). In connection with the preceding surahs, the surah serves as a reminder of the fundamental principles of Islam and transitions to Surah al-Ma'un."